  {
   "name": "Discrete steering pair, slow positive pulse",
   "expr": "Vc = VCC + VfD1",
   "where": {
    "Vc": "voltage reached on the data line",
    "VCC": "supply rail the upper diode returns to",
    "VfD1": "forward voltage of the upper diode D1"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 6
  },
  {
   "name": "Discrete steering pair, slow negative pulse",
   "expr": "Vc = −VfD2",
   "where": {
    "VfD2": "forward voltage of the lower diode D2, which returns to ground"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 6
  },
  {
   "name": "Discrete steering pair, fast positive pulse",
   "expr": "Vc = VCC + Vf + (L diESD/dt)",
   "where": {
    "Vf": "steering diode forward voltage",
    "L": "parasitic inductance of the traces in the current path",
    "diESD/dt": "rate of rise of the ESD current"
   },
   "note": "With a small trace inductance the L·di/dt term alone can reach hundreds of volts on the supply rail.",
   "page": 6
  },
  {
   "name": "Discrete steering pair, fast negative pulse",
   "expr": "Vc = −Vf − (L diESD/dt)",
   "where": {
    "Vf": "steering diode forward voltage",
    "L": "parasitic trace inductance",
    "diESD/dt": "rate of rise of the ESD current"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 6
  },
  {
   "name": "Clamp through the internal surge diode",
   "expr": "Vc = Vf + VRWM",
   "where": {
    "Vf": "forward drop of one steering diode",
    "VRWM": "working voltage of the internal surge diode"
   },
   "note": "Applies with VP open, and to the voltage the protected IC sees when the current is routed through the surge diode.",
   "page": 5
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Internal circuit",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Eight steering diodes D1 to D8 form four series pairs between a common upper node (VP, pin 5) and a common lower node (VN, pin 2); each I/O pin is the junction of one pair, the upper diode's anode and the lower diode's cathode. A surge-rated Zener connects VP to VN, cathode on VP."
  },
  {
   "topic": "How lines are clamped",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "When a line goes above the positive reference by one diode drop (VCC + Vf, or Vf above the Zener) its upper steering diode conducts; when it goes below VN its lower diode conducts. The surge current is thereby diverted around the protected circuit, and on the positive side it flows into the rail or through the Zener to ground. The Zener also keeps the supply rail from being driven into overvoltage. Its clamping voltage depends on the ESD current and is given in the clamping voltage against peak pulse current curve."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Connections",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Data lines go to pins 1, 3, 4 and 6 and the negative reference is pin 2. Three ways to use pin 5: straight to VCC, which also protects the rail and lowers line capacitance through bias (option 1); to VCC through a series resistor, 10 kΩ recommended, which isolates the supply but keeps the bias (option 2); or left unconnected, for boards with no positive reference or with a supply that must be fully isolated (option 3)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Pin 2 goes straight to a ground plane to keep ground inductance low, and PCB traces are kept as short as possible to keep parasitic inductance low; that inductance adds an L·di/dt term to the clamped voltage."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Against discrete diodes",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "A pair of discrete diodes from a data line to the rail and to ground gives the same steering but has drawbacks. Diodes big enough to take the transient have large die and high capacitance, which hurts at high signalling rates; small, low-capacitance die have a higher forward drop at ESD currents and may fail. ESD rise times of a few nanoseconds also make trace inductance matter. SRV05-4 combines fast low-capacitance steering diodes with a surge diode in one package to avoid these issues."
  },
  {
   "topic": "ESD clamping waveforms",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Under an 8 kV contact discharge, a positive strike drives the clamped voltage in a first spike to at least 70 V, then it rings between about 13 V and 25 V for some 30 ns, sags to near 14 V by 100 ns and settles at roughly 8 V to 9 V towards 200 ns. A negative strike spikes to about −52 V, rings between about −5 V and −21 V for roughly 30 ns, and recovers to within a few volts of zero by 200 ns."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Surge waveform",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Surge ratings use the 8 × 20 µs current pulse: the current peaks at IRSM 8 µs after it starts and has fallen to half of IRSM at 20 µs; the pulse width tP is measured to that half-value point."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Construction and marking",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "TSOP-6, case 318G, plastic body with a UL 94 V-0 flammability rating; lead-free. The footprint is compatible with SOT-23 6-lead, SC-59 6-lead and SC-74. Marking is the device code 63, a date code M and a Pb-free dot; the dot may sit in either position and the date-code orientation varies with the assembly site."
  },
  {
